(ns quality.terms
  "The bounded metric terms: erosion and verbosity, both shares in [0, 1]
   where 0 is perfect.

   A SHARE is bounded by construction and strictly monotone in every input.
   That is the property the score needs and the reason the SlopCodeBench
   metrics are shaped this way: unlike a clamped linear, a share has no dead
   zone, so reducing the complexity of the worst function in the corpus
   always moves the number.

   Paper: arXiv 2603.24755 section 2.3. Where we depart from it -- the
   callable enumeration, verbosity being clone-only -- is recorded in
   docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-12-quality-bounded-score-design.md under
   \"Extensions and deviations from SlopCodeBench\"."
  (:require
   [quality.score :as score]))

(defn mass
  "The paper's mass(f) = CC(f) * sqrt(SLOC(f)): complexity weighted by size,
   so a complex 200-line function outweighs an equally complex 3-line one."
  [{:keys [cc sloc]}]
  (* (double cc) (math/sqrt (max 1 (or sloc 1)))))

(defn erosion
  "Erosion = the share of total mass carried by callables above the
   complexity threshold.

       sum over f of 1[CC(f) > 10] * mass(f)   /   sum over f of mass(f)

   The inequality is STRICT: a callable at exactly CC 10 is not
   high-complexity. Returns nil when there is no mass to divide by -- an
   empty corpus is omitted, never reported as a perfect 0.

   `callables` are maps carrying `:cc` and `:sloc`. The enumeration is
   top-level declarations, with live nested functions folded into them; see
   the design's deviations section."
  [callables]
  (let [th (get-in score/thresholds [:erosion :cc])
        total (reduce + 0.0 (map mass callables))]
    (when (pos? total)
      (/ (reduce + 0.0 (map mass (filter (fn [c] (> (:cc c) th)) callables)))
         total))))

(defn verbosity
  "Verbosity = |flagged code lines UNION clone lines| / LOC.

   The paper's numerator unions ast-grep-flagged lines with clone lines. Ours
   flags CODE-verbosity lines only -- comment findings are deliberately NOT
   here, because the paper's rule set is code constructs and its metric is
   introduced as catching copy-pasted or unnecessary lines; comment slop is a
   different thing and lives in `comment-debt`. Folding comments in here
   would be a silent deviation dressed as fidelity, and it would double-count
   against `comment-debt`.

   Still a LOWER BOUND on the paper's figure -- our code rules are a handful
   against their 137, and cover `.lg` only -- but a tighter one than
   clone-only, and it must not be printed against their baselines without
   that caveat.

   `flagged` is a set of [path line] pairs, or nil when the linter could not
   run, in which case only clone lines count and the report must say the
   flagged half was omitted. A UNION, not a sum: a line that is both cloned
   and flagged is one bad line, not two."
  [dup-line-count flagged-count loc]
  (when (and loc (pos? loc))
    (min 1.0 (/ (double (+ (or dup-line-count 0) (or flagged-count 0))) loc))))

(defn comment-debt
  "Flagged comment lines / TOTAL COMMENT LINES.

   OURS, not the paper's. It exists because the paper's denominator is LOC,
   and comments are a small fraction of a codebase -- so a corpus where 11.7%
   of comment lines are flagged shows up as 0.87% of LOC and vanishes into a
   rounding error. Dividing by comment lines puts the finding on its own axis
   at roughly thirteen times the resolution.

   A share like every other term: bounded, monotone, 0 is perfect."
  [flagged-comment-lines total-comment-lines]
  (when (and total-comment-lines (pos? total-comment-lines))
    (min 1.0 (/ (double (or flagged-comment-lines 0)) total-comment-lines))))

(defn squash
  "A rate turned into a bounded term: `v / (v + k)`.

   In [0, 1), zero at zero, strictly monotone, and its derivative is never
   zero -- diminishing returns rather than a cliff. `k` is calibrated so the
   metric's existing `bad` threshold lands on exactly 0.5, which preserves
   the meaning already encoded in `thresholds`."
  [v k]
  (let [v (max 0.0 (double v))]
    (if (pos? (+ v k)) (/ v (+ v k)) 0.0)))

(defn shortfall-squash
  "`squash` for a metric where HIGHER is better: the squash applies to how
   far `v` falls short of `good`, so meeting or beating `good` is exactly 0."
  [v good k]
  (squash (max 0.0 (- (double good) v)) k))

(defn dead-share
  "Dead = SLOC of unreachable definitions / SLOC of all definitions.

   SLOC-weighted, not count-weighted: 200 lines of dead code must read far
   worse than one dead two-liner. Returns nil when there is nothing to
   divide by.

   OURS, NOT THE PAPER'S -- SlopCodeBench never mentions dead code.

   UPPER BOUND. Reachability follows only literally-occurring symbols, so it
   under-approximates reachability and therefore over-approximates dead code:
   anything reached by computed dispatch, a registry, `resolve` or `eval`
   looks dead and is not."
  [dead all]
  (let [total (reduce + 0 (map (fn [d] (or (:sloc d) 1)) all))]
    (when (pos? total)
      (min 1.0 (/ (double (reduce + 0 (map (fn [d] (or (:sloc d) 1)) dead))) total)))))
